        "reason": "The plan's <=0.06 acceptance criterion was WRONG and is replaced. It implicitly assumed the 25-commit COUNT bound (_SWEEP_YIELD_COMMITS) would govern window closure. Measured Stage A commit processing runs ~0.33 s/commit (196.7s converging wall / 600 commits on branch-1), so the 2.0s CLOCK bound (_SWEEP_YIELD_SECONDS) closes each window after roughly 6-8 commits, well before the 25-commit count is ever reached: 72/600 and 75/600 (~0.12-0.125 drops/commit), matching the spike's 70/600 (design doc, 'Evidence this is worth doing'). The clock bound is the hook-lockout bound and is kept deliberately -- it, not the count, is what guarantees a hook is never locked out longer than _SWEEP_YIELD_SECONDS, so lowering it further would trade hook availability for fewer drops. Do not read this as a regression from plan; the ~8x reduction from master's ~1.0 drops/commit to the branch's ~0.12 is the real, load-bearing result, and the ~1/25 figure was never achievable at this repo's per-commit pace without lengthening hook lockout past its budget."
